<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beatrice of Provence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1229–<a href="/years/1267">1267</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-barcelona">House of Barcelona</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Beatrice of Provence was countess of Provence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forcalquier">Forcalquier</a> from 1245, the youngest daughter of Count Ramon Berenguer IV; her three sisters married the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and Richard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>. Her marriage in 1246 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a>, brother of Louis IX, brought Provence to the Angevins, and she was crowned queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> with him in 1266.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beatrice-of-provence.jpg" alt="Beatrice of Provence" width="270" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: en:Cristoforo Orimina · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BeatrixProvensalska.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Beatrice was the fourth and youngest daughter of the Count of Provence, Ramon Berenguer, and Beatrice of Savoy. Her three older sisters all married titles: Margaret became Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as wife of Louis IX, Eleanor Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as wife of Henry III, and Sanchia titular Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> as wife of Richard, Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>. In his will of 20 June 1238, however, Ramon Berenguer unexpectedly left Provence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forcalquier">Forcalquier</a> to Beatrice, still unmarried. After his death on 19 August 1245 she became Countess in her own right, with her mother keeping the usufruct of Provence for life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As a rich heiress Beatrice drew suitors. Raymond VII of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon">James I of Aragon</a>, who invaded Provence, made claims, and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> sent the imperial fleet. Her mother sheltered with her at Aix and appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a>. At a secret meeting at Cluny in December 1245, Innocent, Louis IX, Blanche of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Louis's youngest brother Charles agreed that in return for Louis's military support of the Pope against Frederick, Charles would marry Beatrice. They married at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aix-en-Provence</a> on 31 January 1246.</p><p>Charles refused to share the counties with his sisters-in-law, who felt cheated by their father's will. He also brought in French lawyers, took castles and fees from nobles and shut his mother-in-law out of government. Revolts followed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, and by July 1252 he had defeated them. Beatrice sided with her husband. She went with him on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seventh-crusade">Seventh Crusade</a> in 1248 and bore a first child at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a>, who lived only a few days.</p><p>In 1259 her sister Margaret publicly slighted her by refusing to seat her at the family table, saying Beatrice was not a queen. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-iv">Pope Clement IV</a> granted Charles the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, Beatrice helped raise his army, calling on her knights and, according to the historian Angelo di Costanzo, selling her jewels. Charles was invested as King of Sicily at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 28 June 1265. They were crowned on 6 January 1266, and she held Rome while he fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Beatrice died on 23 September 1267, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nocera-inferiore">Nocera Inferiore</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. The cause is unrecorded, though complications after a pregnancy are suspected. She was buried first in Naples, and in 1277 Charles moved her remains to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aix-en-Provence</a>. As the last ruling Countess of Provence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forcalquier">Forcalquier</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-barcelona">House of Barcelona</a>, she left the counties to her husband. Their children included Charles II, later King of Naples.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Beatrice of Provence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Raymond Berengar IV's daughter <b>Beatrice</b> married Charles of Anjou in 1246, and the Angevin counts, also kings of Naples, ruled from Aix and Naples.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-provence">County of Provence</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nocera-inferiore">Nocera Inferiore</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.75,14.63&amp;year=1253" aria-label="Nocera Inferiore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Beatrice of Provence"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5938 4026h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5938 4026h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.75,14.63&amp;year=1253">What was here in 1253? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iv-count-of-provence"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ramon-berenguer-iv-count-of-provence.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Provence</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; 1198–1245</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/charles-i-of-anjou.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Charles I of Anjou</span><span class="tp">Married &#183; 1226–1285</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-naples"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/charles-ii-of-naples.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Charles II of Naples</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 1254–1309</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
